   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the uniform city court act, the uniform district court act,\nthe uniform justice court act and the New York city civil court act, in\nrelation to obtaining jurisdiction over certain defendants",
    "basePrintNo": "S3468",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3468-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 28,
       "fullName": "Liz Krueger",
       "imgName": "401_liz_krueger.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 401,
       "sessionMemberId": 1206,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "KRUEGER"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§1801 & 1803, UCCA; amd §§1801 & 1803, UDCA; amd §§1801 & 1803, UJCA; amd §§1801 & 1803, NYC Civ Ct Act",
    "lawSection": "Uniform City Court Act",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3468\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the uniform city court act, the\nuniform district court act, the uniform justice court act and the New\nYork city civil court act, in relation to obtaining jurisdiction over\ncertain defendants\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo permit residential tenants fair access to the small claims courts by\nletting them sue in the small claims court located in the area that the\nrental unit is located.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nMakes provisions for small claims court jurisdiction when the claimant\nis or was a tenant or lessee, the defendant is the property owner, the\nclaim relates to such tenancy or lease, and the property is situated\nwithin the county.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrent law places an unfair burden on the tenant in terms of the return\nof their security deposit or other claims, which requires that tenants\nfile a claim in the jurisdiction where the landlord's business is\nlocated. In SOLOMON V. CORRELL, the judge invites the Legislature to\naddress this problem.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2018: S.3316 (Krueger) - Died in Judiciary\n2017: S.3316 (Krueger) - Died in Labor\n2015-16: S.2801 (Krueger) - Died in Judiciary\n2013-14: S.3159 (Krueger) - Died in Judiciary\n2012: S.0441 (Krueger) - Died in Rules\n2011: S.0441 (Krueger) - Died in Judiciary\n2010: S.4781-A (Krueger) - Passed Senate\n2009: S.4781 (Krueger) - Died on Third Reading\n2008: S.7651 (Krueger) - Died in Judiciary\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nOn the first of September next succeeding the date it becomes law, with\nprovisions.\n",
